      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "What sets **Violence** (2005) apart is its documentary-style approach, using handheld cameras and natural lighting to create an immediate, almost journalistic feel. This technique would later influence films like <a href=\"/movie/7347\" class=\"movie-title\">Elite Squad</a> (2007) and <a href=\"/movie/273481\" class=\"movie-title\">Sicario</a> (2015), both of which adopted similar aesthetic choices to portray institutional violence. Mandoki's decision to cast non-professional actors alongside veterans grounds the film in authenticity."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's exploration of cyclical violence echoes through contemporary Mexican cinema. **Amat Escalante's Heli** (2013) and **Michel Franco's New Order** (2020) build on its foundation, examining how violence permeates social structures. The raw energy of **Violence** (2005) can be traced through to recent works like **Tigers Are Not Afraid** (2017) and **La Civil** (2021). SUBHEAD: Impact on Global Crime Cinema"
      },
